WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:06.041
Ok, Neptunium is the first of the synthetically-prepared
heavy elements,

00:00:06.041 --> 00:00:10.880
Uranium being the last naturally-occurring
element. Neptunium is, as you

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.910
might imagine, named after the planet Neptune.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:19.090
The naming of elements is bizarre to say the
least: sometimes it’s who

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.199
discovered it; sometimes it’s where it was
discovered. It has to go to an

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.420
IUPAC naming committee and there’s various,
sort of, rules and

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.520
regulations which come into it.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.230
Synthetics; what do you mean by synthetic
anyway? What is all that

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.230
about?

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.899
Synthetic in the sense that it doesn’t naturally
occur on earth, we’ve had

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.890
to prepare it by taking other heavy elements
then bombarding them with

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:45.570
various nuclei. Now what happens when you
are bombarding nuclei with

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.719
another nuclei is that some bits may stick
and some bits will fall out, but

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:58.020
what you end up with is then a new element-type.
So from here onwards

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.960
everything has been man-made, none of it naturally
occurs, unless you

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.069
count ‘naturally-occurring’ as being found
in the debris of an exploded

00:01:06.069 --> 00:01:06.770
nuclear bomb.

